<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Residual gynecomastia, improving (N62)</content>. Small and non-tender six months after the estrogen stopped. Testosterone 536 ng/dL and estradiol 24 pg/mL are normal. [S]<list><item>Watchful waiting to about twelve months after the estrogen stopped; if the tissue persists and he wants it removed, a plastic surgery opinion, because long-standing gynecomastia becomes fibrous and seldom regresses.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Vitamin D insufficiency, resolved (E55.9)</content>. 25-hydroxyvitamin D 38 ng/mL on cholecalciferol 1,000 units. [S]<list><item>Continue cholecalciferol 1,000 units daily through the winter.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Major depressive disorder, single episode, in remission (F32.1)</content>. PHQ-9 4: remission about six months after starting psychotherapy and four months after adding bupropion. Suicide risk remains low, with protective factors stronger than at intake. [S]<list><item>Continue bupropion XL 150 mg for at least six months after remission, then decide together about tapering.</item><item>Monthly psychotherapy; PHQ-9 every two to three months.</item><item>Collaborative safety plan reviewed and unchanged; 988 Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline and the clinic number confirmed.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Breast examination for size, tenderness, and any discrete mass</td><td>Every visit until the gynecomastia settles</td><td>Completed today</td><td>A soft, non-tender disc about 2 cm on the left and 1 cm on the right; no discrete mass</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Testosterone and estradiol at six months</td><td>Once, to confirm sustained recovery</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Testosterone 536 ng/dL and estradiol 24 pg/mL, normal</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>25-hydroxyvitamin D on replacement</td><td>About three to six months after starting</td><td>Completed today</td><td>38 ng/mL, up from 22</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Surgical opinion for persistent gynecomastia</td><td>If it persists about twelve months and troubles him</td><td>Due</td><td>Not yet: six months since the estrogen stopped and still shrinking</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>PHQ-9</td><td>Baseline, then at least monthly during acute treatment; every 2 to 4 weeks in continuation</td><td>Completed today</td><td>4: remission</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Monthly PHQ-9 between the sixteen-week review and today</td><td>At least monthly during acute treatment</td><td>Deferred</td><td>Not done, a deviation from the protocol: no interim PHQ-9 was scheduled after the response at sixteen weeks, although remission had not been reached, leaving nine weeks unmeasured. No harm resulted judging by today&apos;s review: he reached remission and had no suicidal thoughts, and he saw the psychologist throughout. Corrective action: the clinic registry now books a monthly PHQ-9 call until a remission score is recorded</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>GAD-7</td><td>With the PHQ-9 when anxiety symptoms were present at baseline</td><td>Completed today</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Suicide risk reassessment (C-SSRS Since Last Visit and PHQ-9 item 9)</td><td>Every visit during active treatment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No suicidal thoughts, intent, plan, or behavior since the last visit; PHQ-9 item 9 scored 0. Protective factors stronger than at intake. Safety plan reviewed</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Adherence, adverse effects, and blood pressure on bupropion</td><td>Every visit during active treatment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No missed doses; no side effects; blood pressure 118/72 mmHg</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Psychotherapy, sleep, running, and social contact</td><td>Every visit during active treatment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Course of 16 sessions finished, monthly now; sleep about seven hours; running 40 km a week with his club</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Lithium, anticonvulsant, or antipsychotic monitoring (levels, metabolic tests, AIMS)</td><td>Only on those drugs</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Not on any of these drugs</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Testosterone, LH, and FSH back in the reference range within six months of the last estrogen dose, without testosterone therapy. Outcome by 2026-08-04: Testosterone 452 ng/dL with normal LH and FSH at sixteen weeks [S]</item><item>Breast tenderness gone and gynecomastia reduced; a surgical opinion offered if it persists and troubles him. Outcome by 2026-10-06: Tenderness gone by six months; small residual glandular tissue; watchful waiting to twelve months [S]</item><item>Depression response (PHQ-9 down by at least 50 percent) by sixteen weeks, then remission (PHQ-9 below 5). Outcome by 2026-10-06: PHQ-9 7 at sixteen weeks (down 53 percent) and 4 at six months [S]</item><item>Back to running about 40 km a week and changing at his club again. Outcome by 2026-10-06: Running 40 km a week and back with the club by six months [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: EAA clinical practice guidelines: gynecomastia evaluation and management (2019); Testosterone Therapy in Men With Hypogonadism: An Endocrine Society Clinical Practice Guideline (2018); Osteoporosis in Men: An Endocrine Society Clinical Practice Guideline (2012); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for the Management of Major Depressive Disorder (2022); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for Assessment and Management of Patients at Risk for Suicide (2024); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, depression, bipolar, and mood disorders monograph (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Preventive visit in two weeks; depression review with PHQ-9 in about two months. Next scheduled visit 2026-10-20: periodic adult preventive visit. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
